From: Joan Karadimov <joan.karadimov@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Make isNotifierBusy() give up instead of spinning

Neither failure path in this function leaves it.

    if (!socket.waitForConnected(5000)) { res=false; }   // falls through
    do {
      if (!socket.waitForReadyRead()) { res=false; }     // does not break
      in.startTransaction();
      in >> octopiResponse;
    } while (!in.commitTransaction());

With nothing listening on 127.0.0.1:12702, waitForReadyRead() returns false
immediately, the stream stays empty, commitTransaction() fails, and the loop
runs again -- forever, at full speed, on the GUI thread. octopi shows its
window, finishes loading, then wedges: "Not Responding", one core pinned.

Upstream never reaches this because the guard at the top of the function
always fires there (see 0011), so the whole body is dead code on Linux.
That makes it a real bug rather than a Windows one -- it would bite any
platform where the guard passes and the notifier is not listening.

Worth sending upstream.
diff --git a/src/mainwindow.cpp b/src/mainwindow.cpp
index fc84b31..24b3d70 100644
--- a/src/mainwindow.cpp
+++ b/src/mainwindow.cpp
@@ -169,7 +169,7 @@ bool MainWindow::isNotifierBusy()
 
   if (!socket.waitForConnected(5000))
   {
-    res=false;
+    return false;
   }
 
   QDataStream in(&socket);
@@ -180,7 +180,10 @@ bool MainWindow::isNotifierBusy()
   {
     if (!socket.waitForReadyRead())
     {
-      res=false;
+      // Not breaking here is what made this loop unable to end: nothing was
+      // read, commitTransaction() fails on the empty stream, and waitForReadyRead()
+      // returns false again at once. A dead socket spun the CPU for good.
+      return false;
     }
 
     in.startTransaction();
